---
// src/components/HobbyIslandSection.astro
//
// New section in the `.sky-chain` run, sitting between
// AchievementIslandSection and BonusIslandSection (update the import +
// insertion in index.astro — see the usage example at the bottom of
// this comment).
//
// v2 — reworked after seeing your layered mockup. The three artworks
// (`backIslandImage`, `joyconImage`, `frontIslandImage`) are NOT three
// independent, independently-positioned islands like
// AchievementIslandSection's statue/temple — they're one single
// island illustration split across three files that only line up
// correctly when stacked directly on top of each other, back to
// front, with no relative offset between them.
//
// I confirmed this by feature-matching each of your three original
// uploads against your mockup image (AKAZE + RANSAC homography): all
// three map onto it with the SAME scale (~0.446x) and effectively
// zero relative translation — i.e. your mockup is just "take each
// full original canvas, scale it down by the same amount, lay them
// directly on top of each other." So rather than eyeballing three
// separate `top`/`left` positions (which is what v1 of this file did,
// and why the pieces looked scattered instead of interlocked), the
// three delivered images this time are all cropped from the exact
// SAME rectangle of their original canvases and share IDENTICAL
// pixel dimensions (1900x1028) — stack them with `inset: 0` and they
// land in register automatically, the same way your mockup does:
//
//   1. `backIslandImage`  (bed, first-aid kit, board game, campfire) — bottom layer
//   2. `joyconImage`      (the pink Joy-Con)                          — middle layer
//   3. `frontIslandImage` (fountain, pool, piano, palm tree, ...)     — top layer
//
// IMPORTANT for future edits: if any of these three files ever gets
// re-cropped or re-exported, it has to be cropped from that same
// shared rectangle (or just re-derived from the original uncropped
// canvas at the same scale) — cropping one independently of the
// other two will shift it relative to the others and the island will
// visibly come apart at the seams. There's no per-image `top`/`left`
// tuning in the CSS below to compensate with; alignment is baked into
// the image files themselves, not the layout.
//
// v4 — per your request, the three layers now drift INDEPENDENTLY on
// cursor proximity instead of moving as one rigid unit. Worth calling
// out plainly: this trades away the pixel-perfect interlock from v2/
// v3. At rest (cursor far away, or `prefers-reduced-motion`) all
// three still line up exactly, since every layer's drift settles back
// to `translate(0, 0)` — the seams only start to show once the cursor
// is close enough to actually push them apart. Kept the push small
// (see `data-depth` below) specifically to keep that gap subtle
// rather than letting the artwork visibly tear; turn the depth
// numbers down further (or back to identical) if it reads as too much
// once you see it live.
//
// Each layer gets its own two-level wrapper, same reasoning as v3 for
// why bob and drift can't share an element (a running CSS animation
// silently wins over a JS-set inline `transform` on the same
// property): a `.hobby-layer-bob` wrapper (own bob timing) around a
// `.hobby-layer-drift.parallax-inner` wrapper (own mouse-repel
// strength via `data-depth`). `data-depth` is read by the shared
// script below as a multiplier on the base push distance — back
// island 0.55x (subtle, reads as "further away"), Joy-Con 0.85x,
// front island 1.2x (strongest, reads as "closest to the viewer") —
// a small depth-of-field cue on top of the independence you asked
// for, rather than three identical pushes that just happen to be
// computed separately. Bob timing is also offset per layer (different
// duration + a negative `animation-delay` to start mid-cycle) so they
// don't bob in lockstep either. `joyconImage` is still optional (omit
// it and the other two layers still render/drift fine on their own).
//
// v5 — per your request the island is now A LOT bigger. The previous
// approach (v3/v4) kept the island absolutely positioned, bottom-
// anchored inside a fixed `100vh` section, with `.content` pinned to
// the top — which meant the island's size was always fighting the
// text for a slice of one fixed-height box, and capping it by
// viewport height (`min(..., 86vh)`) to avoid that fight quietly
// capped it fairly small on ordinary monitors too (that's why the
// previous bump barely read as bigger). This version removes that
// fight instead of continuing to referee it: the island is back in
// NORMAL DOCUMENT FLOW, stacked below `.content` (both centered via
// `display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center` on
// `.hobby-section`) rather than absolutely positioned over it. With
// `min-height: 100vh` (not a fixed `height`), the section is simply
// as tall as it needs to be to fit both comfortably — on most screens
// that's now taller than one viewport, so the very bottom of the
// island sits slightly below the fold, which is the expected trade
// for "a lot bigger" rather than a bug. No vh-based caps or
// short-viewport media queries are needed anymore — normal flow can't
// overlap by construction, at any viewport size, so `width` is just a
// plain `clamp()` now.
//
// Background: `hobby_island_background.webp` used to be rendered as a
// FLAT fill (sampled at ≈#01d4f3), close enough to identical to
// AchievementIslandSection's bottom edge / BonusIslandSection's top
// edge that this section always met its neighbors cleanly even without
// any seam-blend help — the shared fade-band system
// (`.seam-fade-top`/`.seam-fade-bottom`) that used to exist for the
// sections where that WASN'T true has since been removed entirely per
// your request, so this note is just historical context for why this
// particular section never needed it.
//
// New: per your request, `.hobby-section`'s background is now a
// top-to-bottom CSS gradient (`#2dddfc` → `#00d4f4`) instead of that
// flat-fill image — see the `.hobby-section` rule's own comment below
// for the swap. `hobby_island_background.webp` itself, and the
// `backgroundImage` prop that points to it, are both left in place
// unused (rather than deleted) in case you ever want to go back to an
// image-based fill here; nothing currently reads the prop's value.
// `#00d4f4` at the bottom is close enough to the old flat fill's
// ≈#01d4f3 that the seam with BonusIslandSection below still meets
// cleanly, same as the note above describes.
//
// v6 — you swapped in your own re-exported CSP layers and the layout
// broke ("look size and position of the site completely changes").
// I checked the three files you sent: back island came in at
// 2002x1035, front island at 3192x1942, Joy-Con at 513x931 — three
// completely different aspect ratios, each unrelated to the other two
// and to the 1900x1028 shared canvas this component was built around.
//
// Root cause: CSP's "export selected layer" trims each layer to its
// own visible-content bounding box before saving. That's exactly what
// v2's top comment warned about — cropping one layer independently of
// the others destroys the relative positioning between them — except
// now it's happening on every export instead of once. Once a layer is
// trimmed, the empty space that encoded "this layer sits 400px lower
// and 200px right of that layer" is just gone from the file. There's
// no metadata recording the trim offset either (checked — none of the
// three files carry it), so there's no way for CODE to reconstruct
// where the layers should sit relative to each other after the fact.
// This genuinely can't be fixed by changing CSS/JS; it has to be fixed
// at export time, by not trimming in the first place.
//
// THE FIX (do this in Clip Studio Paint, takes effect on every future
// export, no code changes ever needed again):
//   Don't use "Export Selected Layer" — that's the command that trims.
//   Instead, for each of the three layers: hide every OTHER layer
//   (toggle their eye icons off) so only the one you want is visible,
//   then use the regular whole-image export you'd use for finished art
//   (File > Export > PNG/JPEG/... — NOT the per-layer export command).
//   That command always exports the full canvas size, transparent
//   wherever nothing's drawn — so if your canvas is 3840x2160, all
//   three exports come out 3840x2160 every time, in the same
//   coordinate system, no matter how much you keep editing the art
//   inside them. Repeat for the other two layers. Drop the three
//   resulting files in as backIslandImage/joyconImage/frontIslandImage
//   — same filenames is fine, no code change needed on your end.
//
// To make THIS FILE robust to that workflow going forward (so if your
// canvas size ever changes — e.g. you resize the CSP document — it's
// a two-number prop edit instead of another round-trip with me), the
// hardcoded `aspect-ratio: 3840 / 2079` is now driven by two new
// OPTIONAL props, `canvasWidth` / `canvasHeight`, defaulting to
// 3840 / 2160 (a plain, untrimmed canvas — no "-79" fudge, since that
// number only existed to match the old, already-cropped 1900x1028
// deliverables). If your actual CSP canvas is a different size, pass
// it in; otherwise leave the props off and the defaults apply.
//
// I can't repair the three files you just sent — the alignment info
// they'd need is already gone from the trimmed images themselves, not
// just missing from the code — so please re-export following the
// steps above and drop the new files in; everything else about this
// component (stacking, independent drift, bob, sizing) stays exactly
// as it was.
//
// v7 — added an optional `backIslandFrozenImage` layer that overlays
// the back island and periodically "reveals" itself (spreads out from
// its own center, holds for a few seconds, then withdraws back to the
// center the same way) at random intervals, fully hands-off once it's
// on the page. SUPERSEDED BY v12 BELOW: the random-timer trigger
// described in point 3 is gone — it's a hover reveal now. Points 1 and
// 2 (alignment, the circle-reveal mechanic) still apply as-is; left
// this section intact rather than rewriting it, same as how v2/v3
// stay in place after later versions changed that behavior too. Three
// implementation notes (again, point 3 is historical):
//
//   1. Alignment: this overlay lives INSIDE the back island's own
//      `.hobby-layer-drift` wrapper (not a sibling slot like Joy-Con/
//      front island), stacked directly on top of `backIslandImage`
//      with `position: absolute; inset: 0`. That means it moves and
//      bobs together with the back island as one piece (correct — it's
//      frost ON the island, not a separate floating object), and it
//      only lines up correctly if it's exported at the exact same
//      canvas size as the rest (see v6) — confirmed your upload is
//      3840x2160, matching.
//
//   2. The "spreads out from the center, holds, withdraws" motion is a
//      `clip-path: circle()` reveal, not a scale/opacity transform —
//      animating the circle's radius from 0% to comfortably past the
//      image's farthest corner (150%, overshooting on purpose so the
//      exact aspect ratio doesn't matter) and back reads as the frost
//      actually spreading across the existing artwork, rather than the
//      whole overlay image growing/fading in place.
//
//   3. "From time to time (randomly)" is handled entirely in the
//      script below with `setTimeout` + `Math.random()` — no fixed
//      loop/interval, so appearances don't fall into a predictable
//      rhythm. Each cycle: wait a random 8-22s, play the ~6.5s reveal-
//      hold-withdraw animation once, then pick a new random wait and
//      repeat, forever, starting from when the page loads. Skipped
//      entirely under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, consistent
//      with how the bob/drift are already handled elsewhere in this
//      file. Omit the prop and nothing changes — no overlay element is
//      even rendered.
//
// v8 — added a hover swap on the palm tree: `palmTreeImage` (pulled
// out of `frontIslandImage` into its own layer) is the default, and
// hovering it cross-fades in `palmTree80sImage` with a top-to-bottom
// wipe, reversing the same way on mouse-out. A few things worth
// calling out:
//
//   - Both new images are OPTIONAL and layer inside the FRONT island's
//     existing `.hobby-layer-drift` (same reasoning as the v7 frozen
//     overlay on the back island: it needs to bob/drift together with
//     the rest of that island, not move independently), stacked in
//     this order: `frontIslandImage`, then `palmTreeImage`, then the
//     hover hotspot, then `palmTree80sImage`. Both tree images must be
//     exported at the same canvas size as everything else (see v6) —
//     confirmed your two uploads are both 3840x2160.
//
//   - The reveal itself is a plain CSS `clip-path: inset()` transition
//     driven by `:hover` — no JS state machine needed. At rest,
//     `palmTree80sImage` is clipped away entirely except a 0px sliver
//     at its own top edge (`inset(0 0 100% 0)`); hovering animates
//     that to `inset(0 0 0% 0)`, i.e. the clipped-away region shrinks
//     from the bottom up, which reads as the 80s art wiping DOWN over
//     the normal tree. Un-hovering just transitions back, so the wipe
//     reverses on its own — no separate "hide" animation to maintain.
//
//   - The trickier part: `.hobby-island-group` has `pointer-events:
//     none` on it (so none of this artwork ever intercepts clicks on
//     the page, and so the mouse-repel drift's own `mousemove`
//     listener — which reads plain viewport coordinates, not real
//     hover state — isn't affected). Naively adding `:hover` to one of
//     the tree images directly wouldn't fire at all because of that.
//     Also, using the tree's full layer box (the whole `inset: 0` slot
//     — mostly transparent canvas padding) as the hover target would
//     trigger from anywhere near the front island, not just "when you
//     moved over" the tree itself. Both problems are solved by one
//     small invisible `.hobby-palm-hotspot` div, explicitly opted back
//     into `pointer-events: auto` (an element CAN re-enable pointer
//     events even under an ancestor's `pointer-events: none` — it's
//     not inherited as a hard block, just a default), sized to the
//     tree's actual painted pixels rather than its canvas-sized
//     bounding box.
//
//   - That sizing is computed at runtime, not hardcoded: the script
//     below draws `palmTreeImage` to an offscreen canvas once it's
//     loaded, scans its alpha channel for the real ink bounding box,
//     and positions the hotspot from that (as percentages, so it still
//     tracks correctly at any rendered size). Given how often you're
//     re-exporting these layers, hand-picked percentages would've gone
//     stale the next time the tree moves or resizes within the canvas
//     — this way there's nothing to keep in sync by hand, ever, same
//     spirit as v6/v7. Needs `palmTreeImage` to be same-origin (true
//     for your site's own `/images/` assets) since cross-origin canvas
//     pixel reads throw; that's caught, and the hotspot just falls
//     back to covering the whole slot rather than breaking anything.
//
// v9 — v8's reveal was a pure overlay: `palmTree80sImage` wiped in on
// TOP of `palmTreeImage`, which stayed fully opaque underneath the
// whole time. Since the 80s art isn't the same silhouette as the
// normal tree (different fronds, no Kirby, wider canopy), the two
// don't mask each other pixel-for-pixel, so bits of the normal tree
// showed through wherever the 80s layer above it was transparent —
// exactly what you flagged. Fixed by making `.hobby-palm-base` clip
// itself away in lockstep with `.hobby-palm-80s` revealing, using the
// same `inset()` property, direction, duration, and easing on both —
// so the region the 80s tree has revealed so far is ALWAYS exactly
// the region the normal tree has just clipped away, with the boundary
// itself moving as one edge instead of two independent wipes that
// happen to overlap. At any instant exactly one image is visible at
// each point on the tree; never both, never neither. Also added a
// thin synthwave-gradient "scanline" that sweeps down with it (a
// one-shot `animation`, not a `transition`, since it needs to fade in
// AND back out rather than settle at an end state) — the glowing
// leading edge is what pushes this from "wipe" to "80s computery
// digitizing effect," which is what you asked for instead of a plain
// cross-fade/reveal.
//
// v10 — two fixes to that scanline, per your feedback (full reasoning
// is inline as a comment right above `.hobby-palm-scanline` below):
// it was spanning the whole front island instead of just the tree
// (moved it from a sibling of the hotspot to a CHILD of it, so it
// inherits the hotspot's already-correct tree-sized box for free), and
// it's now a thinner cyan/magenta hairline pair with a stepped
// (not smooth) sweep for a more "electronic" feel, per your request.
//
// v11 — pushed "more electronic" further with two additions, both
// full reasoning inline near the relevant rules below: a brightness/
// hue flicker on `palmTree80sImage` itself as it locks in (a signal-
// settling stutter, only on the way in — the normal tree still just
// clips cleanly away), and an irregular horizontal jitter on the scan
// line as it sweeps down (signal noise, not a deliberate wobble). Both
// ride on top of the existing v9/v10 mechanics rather than replacing
// anything — the clip-path sync that guarantees no double-exposure
// and the tree-scoped hotspot positioning are untouched.
//
// v12 — the back island frost (v7) used to appear on a random timer;
// now it's a hover reveal instead, matching the palm tree pattern
// (v8): a `.hobby-frost-hotspot` div, alpha-scanned to the frost art's
// own painted pixels (not the whole back island), and
// `.hobby-frozen-overlay` transitions its `clip-path` on `:hover`
// instead of running a fixed 6.5s keyframe loop — it grows while
// you're over the hotspot and withdraws the moment you leave, for as
// long as the hover lasts, rather than a timed appear-hold-withdraw
// cycle running on its own. The random-interval `setTimeout` scheduler
// from v7 is gone entirely. Along the way, refactored the alpha-scan
// logic (used to size the palm tree's hotspot) into a shared
// `fitHotspotToAlpha()` helper in the script below, since it's now
// doing the same job twice — same behavior as before for the palm
// tree, just de-duplicated rather than copy-pasted a second time for
// the frost hotspot.
//
// v13 — slowed the frost hover transition from 0.6s to 1.1s, per your
// feedback. Only the frost — the palm tree hover swap stays at 0.6s.
// Slowed again in v16, from 1.1s to 1.8s, per further feedback (see the
// `.hobby-frozen-overlay` comment below).
//
// v14 — added the Monado sword: `monadoImage` is a plain new layer,
// LAST in the front island's stack so it renders in front of
// everything else there (per "part of the front island in the front
// of it"). `monadoSymbolImages` is an array of interchangeable symbol
// images for the sword's gem — I checked your five uploads and they
// all share the identical painted bounding box, so this needed no
// hotspot/alignment work at all (unlike palm tree/frost): just one
// `.is-active` symbol shown at a time, swapping to a different random
// one at a random 3-7s interval, forever, starting immediately on
// load so the gem is never empty. Full reasoning for the flicker-in
// swap (and why the outgoing symbol gets a hard cut instead of a
// fade, to avoid the same double-exposure problem v9 solved for the
// palm tree) is inline near `.hobby-monado-symbol` below.
//
// v15 — split the single shared `.hobby-island-group` into two
// INDEPENDENTLY positioned groups (back-group, front-group) that flank
// `.content`, per your mockup screenshots — closer to how
// AchievementIslandSection's statue/temple work, and a deliberate walk
// -back of the v2/v3 rule that back/joycon/front had to share one box
// and could never move independently. You're now explicitly asking for
// two separate islands with their own position rather than one artwork
// that happens to be split across three files, so that constraint no
// longer applies. This needed no new image exports: every layer is
// already exported at the full, untrimmed, IDENTICAL canvas size (see
// v6), so each group just gets its own full `canvasWidth`/
// `canvasHeight` box (same aspect-ratio math as before) instead of
// sharing one — no cropping, no new coordinates to compute.
//
//   - `joyconImage` went into the back-group. Judgment call: your
//     mockup screenshots don't show it distinctly, so there was nothing
//     to match against — but thematically the Joy-Con reads closer to
//     the back island's "games night" set (bed, board game, campfire)
//     than the front island's fountain/pool/piano, so it stays there.
//     Easy to move to the front-group instead if that guess is wrong —
//     it's just the `hobby-layer-slot-joycon` block moving from one
//     group's markup to the other's.
//
//   - Desktop (the default, no media query): both groups are pulled out
//     of `.hobby-section`'s flex flow with `position: absolute` and
//     anchored to opposite corners (back-group upper-left, front-group
//     lower-right — matching the mockup) with a small negative offset
//     so they hang slightly off the section's edge rather than sitting
//     fully inside it, similar to the wave/shape bleeding off-frame in
//     your screenshots. With both islands out of flow, `.content`
//     becomes the ONLY normal-flow child left in the flex column, so it
//     just centers itself with nothing above/below pushing it around
//     anymore — that's what closes up the empty space you flagged,
//     no manual repositioning of `.content` itself needed.
//
//   - Mobile (`max-width: 640px`): both groups go back to NORMAL FLOW
//     instead of `position: absolute`. The markup below is already in
//     back-group → content → front-group order specifically so this
//     falls out for free — one island above the text, one below, no
//     `order` property needed. Both groups also go oversized
//     (`min(150vw, 900px)`, centered with `margin-inline: auto`), which
//     is what makes them "cut off on the left/right sides" like you
//     asked — the same horizontal-bleed trick already relied on
//     elsewhere in this file (see the `overflow-x: hidden` note on
//     `html` in Layout.astro, referenced in the `.hobby-section`
//     comment below), just applied per-island now instead of to one
//     shared group.
//
//   - First pass at this shrank each group's `width` down to roughly a
//     third of the original combined size, which read as "the islands
//     got tiny" — not what you asked for. Fixed: each group's `width`
//     is now the exact same `clamp(700px, 82vw, 1600px)` the single
//     shared box used before v15, so the on-screen size of each island
//     is back to what it was — only its POSITION changed (corner-
//     anchored instead of centered/stacked). Worth flagging since it
//     falls out of keeping that size at this width: on shorter desktop
//     viewports the two corner-anchored boxes can now reach far enough
//     toward the middle to sit close behind (or slightly under)
//     `.content` — `.content`'s `z-index: 2` keeps the text itself
//     always readable on top, but if that closeness reads as too much
//     once you see it with your real art (which likely has different
//     padding around the painted pixels than my placeholder test
//     images), let me know and I can pull the anchors further out or
//     give the section more vertical room, rather than shrinking the
//     islands again.
//
// v17 — exactly that flagged risk happened once you tried it on real
// screens: both islands read as too big on wide viewports, and the
// front island was sitting behind `.content`, making the text harder
// to read. Two changes, desktop only (`min-width: 641px`) — mobile is
// untouched:
//
//   1. Both groups' `width` shrunk from `clamp(700px, 82vw, 1600px)`
//      to `clamp(560px, 60vw, 1150px)` — roughly a 25-30% reduction at
//      every viewport width, not just the extremes.
//
//   2. The front island now sits BELOW `.content` instead of corner-
//      anchored near it. Getting this right needed more than a
//      hardcoded position, though: `.content`'s height isn't fixed —
//      it changes with your actual title/body copy length, viewport
//      width (line-wrapping), and font-size `clamp()`s — so any fixed
//      percentage I picked would only be correct for THIS placeholder
//      copy at THIS viewport, and drift out of sync (overlapping again)
//      the moment either changes. Same reasoning as `fitHotspotToAlpha`
//      elsewhere in this file: measure the real thing at runtime rather
//      than hardcode a guess. New script logic (see
//      `positionFrontGroupBelowContent()` below) measures `.content`'s
//      actual rendered bottom edge and sets the front island's `top`
//      that many px below it plus a fixed gap, clearing the old
//      `bottom` anchor so the two don't fight (an absolutely positioned
//      box with BOTH `top` and `bottom` set and no explicit height
//      stretches to fill the space between them, which would have
//      broken the aspect-ratio-driven sizing). Re-runs on resize, on
//      the mobile/desktop breakpoint changing, and once web fonts
//      finish loading (text can reflow taller/shorter right after fonts
//      swap in). On mobile, or if this script fails for any reason, it
//      falls back to the plain CSS position below `.content` in normal
//      flow (mobile) — nothing breaks, it just stops being able to
//      track `.content`'s exact height on desktop specifically.
//
// v18 — the "closer to the text" fix from the previous round barely
// read as a change once you tried it, even after shrinking the fixed
// gap from 32px down to 4px. Root cause: `frontIslandImage`'s canvas has
// its own chunk of transparent padding above the actual painted art
// (same kind of thing `fitHotspotToAlpha` already accounts for on the
// palm tree/frost hotspots, just never applied to this particular
// measurement) — the OLD math was positioning the top of that mostly-
// invisible BOX a fixed gap below the text, so shrinking the gap was
// mostly closing empty space nobody could see, not moving the actual
// fountain/stairs artwork closer. Fixed by alpha-scanning
// `frontIslandBaseImg` once (reusing the same scan now shared with
// `fitHotspotToAlpha` — see `scanAlphaBBoxFraction` in the script
// below) to find exactly how much of the box's top is transparent, and
// subtracting that from the computed position — so "4px gap" now means
// 4px below the VISIBLE art, not 4px below the box. Also updated
// `showFrontGroupOnceReady()` to wait for this scan too (alongside web
// fonts) before its first reveal, so the v17 anti-jump fix still holds:
// nothing appears until the FINAL, fully-corrected position is known.
//
// v19 — added an optional `fountainImage` layer (part of the front
// island) with an animated "water flowing" effect: thin streaks that
// loop continuously downward, giving the still artwork the look of
// moving water. `fountainImage` itself renders as a normal stacked
// layer (same pattern as every other optional layer here), and a
// second element, `.hobby-fountain-flow`, sits on top of it as a
// repeating gradient animated via `background-position`. The part that
// makes this work cleanly: that overlay is `mask-image`'d to
// `fountainImage`'s OWN alpha shape (via the `--fountain-mask` custom
// property, set inline per-instance in the markup) — so the streaks
// are clipped to exactly where the water is, never bleeding onto the
// surrounding island art, regardless of how the fountain's shape
// changes if you re-export it. `mix-blend-mode: screen` lightens
// rather than painting flat white bars, so it reads as light moving
// across water rather than stripes overlaid on top. Purely ambient/
// decorative — no hover, no interaction — so like the palm tree
// scanline it's cut entirely under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`
// rather than frozen mid-animation. Optional: omit the prop and
// nothing extra renders.
//
// Carries `.sky-chain` (collapses the gap on both sides) and
// `.halftone-hover` (opts into the single page-wide cursor-spotlight
// element from Layout.astro) — same as every other section in the
// chain, no extra markup/script needed for either.
//
// v19 fix — `.hobby-fountain-image` (the `fountainImage` `<img>` itself)
// was missing `position: absolute; inset: 0`, so it stayed in normal
// document flow and stacked BELOW `frontIslandImage` instead of
// overlaying it, adding a second full-canvas-height box under the front
// island. Fixed to match `.hobby-palm-base`/`.hobby-monado`'s treatment.
//
// v19 tweak — water darker/more saturated via a `filter` on
// `.hobby-fountain-image` (no re-export of the art needed), and the
// streak gradient's soft-fade stops swapped for hard-edged stop pairs
// so the lines read as crisp bands instead of a blurred glow.
//
// v20 — the streaks were still perfectly straight bands; per your
// feedback ("a bit curved... more realistic"), `.hobby-fountain-flow`'s
// background swapped from a `repeating-linear-gradient` to a small
// tiling SVG data-URI containing one gentle wave-shaped stroke per
// tile (`background-repeat: repeat`, fixed px tile size so the wave
// period stays consistent regardless of the fountain's rendered size).
// Same masking/animation approach as before (`mask-image` to the
// fountain's own alpha shape, `background-position` looping downward)
// — only the shape of the line itself changed, from ruler-straight to
// a soft S-curve, which reads more like light bending across a curved
// water surface.
//
// v20 tweak — per your reference image, that S-curve tiled several
// small ripples across the width; you wanted ONE wide, shallow arc
// instead (like the smile-shaped curve in your screenshot), echoing
// the fountain rim's own curve. First attempt stretched a single arc
// across the full front-island box (`background-size: 100%` +
// `repeat-y`) — but the fountain's visible area is only a small slice
// of that box, so the arc was barely visible through the mask. Fixed
// by sizing the tile in real pixels close to the fountain's typical
// rendered width instead, tiling both axes so a full "smile"
// consistently lands within the visible water. Same looping
// `background-position` animation as before.
//
// v21 — the aligned single arc (v20 tweak) STILL didn't read right to
// you, and at that point we'd tried three different "line" shapes
// (straight bands, small ripples, one wide arc) without landing on
// something that actually looked good — a sign the "draw a line that
// traces the water's edge" idea itself was the wrong direction, not
// just its execution. Replaced entirely with a different effect:
// several soft, blurred highlights (plain CSS `radial-gradient`s, no
// SVG this time) that drift and re-overlap in a loose, non-repeating-
// feeling loop, like light dancing on moving water (real water doesn't
// have "lines" so much as shifting patches of brightness). This also
// meant the v20 alpha-scan sizing script (`sizeFountainFlowToAlpha()`,
// `--fountain-arc-width`/`--fountain-arc-left`) is gone — the whole
// point of that machinery was aligning a line's shape/position to the
// water's geometry, which a soft drifting blob doesn't need: it looks
// right at any size/position within the mask, no measurement required.
// Masking (`--fountain-mask`, unchanged since v19) still confines it to
// exactly the water's own alpha shape.
//
// v21 tweak / tweak 2 — softened the drifting highlights (lower peak
// opacity, a `blur`, drift keyframes pulled off the mask's edges) after
// an occasional hard-looking cutoff, then switched `mix-blend-mode`
// from `screen` to `color` after picking more distinct hues still
// wasn't a "major" enough difference — `screen` only brightens, which
// converges any highlight color toward the same pale result on an
// already-saturated backdrop, so `color` (recolors the backdrop's
// hue/saturation, keeps its shading) was the actual fix, not the color
// choices themselves.
//
// v22 — per your ask for something more creative, first tried
// concentric ripple rings (`.hobby-fountain-ripple` × 3, masked +
// recolored like v21). Properly diagnosed this time by pausing every
// other animation and diffing a frame with/without it — genuinely zero
// pixel difference, not just subtle. Rather than keep guessing at
// numbers against a mask I can't see for real, switched to a different
// KIND of effect entirely: an SVG `feTurbulence`/`feDisplacementMap`
// filter that visibly warps `fountainImage`'s own pixels (a "wobbling
// water" distortion, not a color overlay on top) — see the CSS comment
// near `.hobby-fountain-image` for the full reasoning. This can't miss
// the water the way an overlay can, since it distorts the water's own
// already-correctly-placed pixels directly, no separate positioning or
// masking involved. `--fountain-mask` (still used by nothing now that
// there's no separate overlay layer) and the alpha-scan sizing script
// were removed as dead weight.
//
// v23 — the wobble "didn't work that well" either, and you asked for a
// specifically DIGITAL/glitch look rather than another organic-water
// attempt: horizontal clip-path "tear" slices + red/cyan chromatic-
// aberration ghost layers, same visual language as the palm tree's
// existing 80s glitch swap (v10/v11), applied to the water.
//
// v24 — you correctly flagged that v23 read as the water's SHAPE
// moving around rather than a digital effect confined inside it —
// clip-path-slicing the whole water layer and shifting it sideways
// moves its silhouette, not just its content. Back to `mask-image`
// (`--fountain-mask`) so the effect's outer edge is pinned exactly to
// the water's real boundary and never moves, with a tiling pattern of
// short vertical ticks (per your ask for "lines going top to bottom")
// in three accent colors, scrolling downward on a `steps()` timing so
// it reads as jerky/digital rather than smooth-flowing water — see the
// CSS comment near `.hobby-fountain-digital` for the full design.
//
// v25 — per your feedback, the ticks are now horizontal (not vertical)
// and three shades of BLUE rather than cyan/pink/mint — picked to
// differ in hue specifically, not just lightness, since `mix-blend-
// mode: color` doesn't carry lightness differences through the way it
// does hue (see the CSS comment near `.hobby-fountain-digital`).
//
// v26 — per your ask for "full long lines, maybe glitchy, something
// similar to the glitch effect in achievement island" (with the full
// `AchievementIslandSection.astro` source pasted as reference): the
// tick pattern is now full-width `repeating-linear-gradient` bands
// (genuinely full-length lines, not short dashes), and a second
// animation layered on top applies `AchievementIslandSection.astro`'s
// own `clip-path`-banding + `filter: hue-rotate()/saturate()` jump-cut
// technique (`steps(1, jump-end)`, same keyframe shape as its
// `glitch-pop`), plus a scanline `::after` flicker mirroring its
// `glitch-scan`. `mask-image` (`--fountain-mask`, unchanged since v19)
// still pins the effect strictly inside the water's own silhouette —
// see the CSS comment near `.hobby-fountain-digital` for why this
// avoids v23's "shape moves" regression.
//
// v27 — the v26 glitch read "a bit weird" per your feedback; per your
// new reference image, replaced it with small white sparkle/glint
// highlights that twinkle on and off across the water's surface,
// dropping the line/band/glitch concept entirely. `.hobby-fountain-
// digital` (and its whole v24-v26 lineage) is gone; `.hobby-fountain-
// sparkle` (still masked to `--fountain-mask`, unchanged since v19)
// now wraps several `.hobby-fountain-sparkle-dot` spans — each a small
// glow + crossed-bar "flare" shape that fades in/out independently on
// its own timing — see the CSS comment near `.hobby-fountain-sparkle`
// for the full design.
//
// v28 — per your feedback, added a small drift to each sparkle so the
// highlights genuinely move ("white dots moving a bit around"),
// suggesting light glinting on FLOWING water rather than fixed
// twinkling points. Folded into the same `hobby-fountain-sparkle-
// twinkle` keyframe via two new per-dot custom properties, `--dx`/
// `--dy` — see the CSS comment near `.hobby-fountain-sparkle` for the
// full reasoning.
//
// v29 — per your feedback, it "still looks like stars": the four-
// pointed crossed-bar flare shape on each dot (v27) was the actual
// culprit, not the color or motion. Removed the rays entirely and
// swapped the perfect circle for an irregular blurred blob (asymmetric
// `border-radius`) — see the CSS comment near `.hobby-fountain-
// sparkle-dot` for the full reasoning.
//
// v30 — per your feedback, the highlights should be CRISP, not soft:
// dropped v29's `blur` and tightened the gradient falloff so each blob
// has a short, defined edge rather than a long soft fade — see the CSS
// comment near `.hobby-fountain-sparkle-dot` for the full reasoning.
//
// v31 — per your reference screenshot of the actual fountain art (with
// its own painted highlight shapes visible), redesigned to match that
// style directly: two round-dot-plus-oval pairs on one side of the
// water (not nine scattered blobs), flat solid white capsules instead
// of gradient-glow blobs, and pure up/down `translateY` bobbing instead
// of the old diagonal drift + fade — see the CSS comment near `.hobby-
// fountain-sparkle-dot` for the full reasoning.
//
// v32 — per your feedback, removed the two small round dots (keeping
// just the two wider ovals) and moved the top oval further left/inward
// so it renders fully within the fountain's silhouette instead of
// getting clipped at the mask's edge — see the CSS comment near
// `.hobby-fountain-sparkle-dot` for the full reasoning.
//
// Usage — insert in src/pages/index.astro between
// AchievementIslandSection and BonusIslandSection:
//
//   import HobbyIslandSection from '../components/HobbyIslandSection.astro';
//
//   <HobbyIslandSection
//     id="section-hobbies"
//     eyebrow="Island 05"
//     title="Ut Labore Et Dolore"
//     text="Sed ut perspiciatis unde omnis iste natus error sit voluptatem accusantium doloremque laudantium, totam rem aperiam."
//     backIslandImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_back_island.webp"
//     joyconImage="/personal-cv-page/images/joycon.webp"
//     frontIslandImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_front_island.webp"
//     backgroundImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_island_background.webp"
//     backIslandFrozenImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_back_island_frozen.webp"
//     palmTreeImage="/personal-cv-page/images/palm_tree.webp"
//     palmTree80sImage="/personal-cv-page/images/palm_tree_80s.webp"
//     monadoImage="/personal-cv-page/images/monado.webp"
//     monadoSymbolImages={[
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_1.webp",
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_2.webp",
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_3.webp",
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_4.webp",
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_5.webp",
//     ]}
//     fountainImage="/personal-cv-page/images/fountain.webp"
//     canvasWidth={3840}
//     canvasHeight={2160}
//   />

// New: every image prop below is an imported src/images/ module
// (`ImageMetadata`) instead of a public/images/ URL string, rendered
// via <Image /> instead of a plain <img> — see src/pages/index.astro's
// frontmatter note for why. `fountainImage` is the one prop used in
// TWO places: the visual <Image /> layer itself, and as the source of
// the `--fountain-mask` CSS custom property (a `url(...)`) used to
// clip the sparkle animation to the fountain's own alpha shape.
// <Image /> can't do that second, CSS-only job (it's an element, not
// a URL), so `getImage()` — the same astro:assets pipeline underneath
// <Image /> — runs it through Astro's optimizer once, up front, and
// `optimizedFountainImage.src` below feeds the mask.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import type { ImageMetadata } from 'astro:assets';

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  backIslandImage: ImageMetadata;
  joyconImage?: ImageMetadata;
  frontIslandImage: ImageMetadata;
  backgroundImage: ImageMetadata;
  /** Optional overlay that periodically reveals itself on top of the
   *  back island (see the v7 comment above). Must be exported at the
   *  same canvas size as the other layers to line up. Omit it and no
   *  overlay element is rendered at all. */
  backIslandFrozenImage?: ImageMetadata;
  /** Optional palm tree layer, pulled out of `frontIslandImage` (see
   *  the v8 comment above). Renders as a normal part of the front
   *  island if `palmTree80sImage` is omitted; pass both to enable the
   *  hover swap. Same canvas-size requirement as every other layer. */
  palmTreeImage?: ImageMetadata;
  /** Optional hover-alternate for `palmTreeImage` — wipes in top-to-
   *  bottom on hover, reverses on mouse-out. Ignored if `palmTreeImage`
   *  isn't also provided. */
  palmTree80sImage?: ImageMetadata;
  /** Optional Monado sword layer, sitting in FRONT of the rest of the
   *  front island (see the v14 comment above). Same canvas-size
   *  requirement as every other layer. */
  monadoImage?: ImageMetadata;
  /** Optional array of symbol images for the sword's gem — one is
   *  shown at a time, swapping to a random different one at random
   *  intervals for as long as the page is open. All must share the
   *  exact same painted position (yours do — same bounding box on all
   *  five). Ignored if `monadoImage` isn't also provided. */
  monadoSymbolImages?: ImageMetadata[];
  /** Optional fountain/water layer, part of the front island (see the
   *  v19 comment above) — gets an animated "flowing water" effect
   *  (looping streaks masked to this image's own alpha shape, so they
   *  never spill outside the water). Same canvas-size requirement as
   *  every other layer. */
  fountainImage?: ImageMetadata;
  /** Your CSP document's canvas size in px — every layer export must
   *  share this exact size (see the v6 comment above). Defaults to
   *  3840x2160; override only if your actual CSP canvas differs. */
  canvasWidth?: number;
  canvasHeight?: number;
}

const {
  id,
  eyebrow,
  title,
  text,
  backIslandImage,
  joyconImage,
  frontIslandImage,
  backgroundImage,
  backIslandFrozenImage,
  palmTreeImage,
  palmTree80sImage,
  monadoImage,
  monadoSymbolImages,
  fountainImage,
  canvasWidth = 3840,
  canvasHeight = 2160,
} = Astro.props;
const optimizedFountainImage = fountainImage
  ? await getImage({ src: fountainImage })
  : undefined;
---

<section
  class="hobby-section sky-chain halftone-hover"
  id={id}
>
  <div class="hobby-island-group hobby-island-group-back" aria-hidden="true">
    <div
      class="hobby-island-group-inner"
      style={`aspect-ratio: ${canvasWidth} / ${canvasHeight};`}
    >
      <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-back">
        <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-back">
          <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="0.55">
            <Image class="hobby-layer" src={backIslandImage} alt="" />
            {backIslandFrozenImage && (
              <>
                <div class="hobby-frost-hotspot" data-frost-hotspot></div>
                <Image
                  class="hobby-layer hobby-frozen-overlay"
                  src={backIslandFrozenImage}
                  alt=""
                  data-frozen-overlay
                />
              </>
            )}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {joyconImage && (
        <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-joycon">
          <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-joycon">
            <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="0.85">
              <Image class="hobby-layer" src={joyconImage} alt="" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    {/* set:html here because hobby text is a real HTML list with a link
       (see the "hobby" dictionary entry in src/i18n/ui.ts) -- <div>,
       not <p>, since a <ul> can't nest inside a <p>. */}
    <div class="body-text" set:html={text} />
  </div>

  <div class="hobby-island-group hobby-island-group-front" aria-hidden="true">
    <div
      class="hobby-island-group-inner"
      style={`aspect-ratio: ${canvasWidth} / ${canvasHeight};`}
    >
      <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-front">
        <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-front">
          <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="1.2">
            <Image
              class="hobby-layer"
              src={frontIslandImage}
              alt=""
              data-front-island-base
            />
            {fountainImage && (
              <>
                <Image
                  class="hobby-layer hobby-fountain-image"
                  src={fountainImage}
                  alt=""
                  data-fountain-image
                />
                <div
                  class="hobby-fountain-sparkle"
                  aria-hidden="true"
                  style={`--fountain-mask: url(${optimizedFountainImage?.src});`}
                  data-fountain-sparkle
                >
                  <span class="hobby-fountain-sparkle-dot" data-fx="0.62" data-fy="0.17" style="--sd: 0.5s; --sdur: 3.4s; --sw: 34px; --sh: 15px; --by: 4px;"></span>
                  <span class="hobby-fountain-sparkle-dot" data-fx="0.68" data-fy="0.56" style="--sd: 0.3s; --sdur: 3.2s; --sw: 38px; --sh: 16px; --by: 4px;"></span>
                </div>
              </>
            )}
            {palmTreeImage && palmTree80sImage && (
              <div class="hobby-palm-hotspot" data-palm-hotspot>
                <div class="hobby-palm-scanline" aria-hidden="true"></div>
              </div>
            )}
            {palmTreeImage && (
              <Image
                class="hobby-layer hobby-palm-base"
                src={palmTreeImage}
                alt=""
                data-palm-base
              />
            )}
            {palmTreeImage && palmTree80sImage && (
              <Image
                class="hobby-layer hobby-palm-80s"
                src={palmTree80sImage}
                alt=""
              />
            )}
            {monadoImage && (
              <Image class="hobby-layer hobby-monado" src={monadoImage} alt="" />
            )}
            {monadoImage &&
              monadoSymbolImages &&
              monadoSymbolImages.map((src) => (
                <Image
                  class="hobby-layer hobby-monado-symbol"
                  src={src}
                  alt=""
                  data-monado-symbol
                />
              ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-free mouse-repel drift as the rest of this family
  // (see AchievementIslandSection.astro / ComputerIslandSection.astro
  // for the original), plus this section's own front-island
  // positioning, hover hotspots, and Monado symbol cycling below --
  // all wrapped in `astro:page-load` (fires on the initial load AND
  // after every <ClientRouter /> navigation -- see
  // src/layouts/Layout.astro) instead of running at the top level,
  // since a language switch swaps in a freshly-parsed page and this
  // module itself only executes once. `cleanupHobby` tears down the
  // previous navigation's rAF loop, `resize`/`mousemove`/matchMedia
  // listeners, and pending Monado-cycle timer first, so switching
  // languages back and forth all session never piles up duplicates of
  // any of them running at once (`window`/`document`, unlike the
  // elements queried below, are never recreated by a soft navigation).
  let cleanupHobby: (() => void) | null = null;

  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => {
    cleanupHobby?.();

    const controller = new AbortController();
    const { signal } = controller;
    let parallaxRafId: number | null = null;
    const monadoTimeoutHolder: { id: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null } = {
      id: null,
    };

    const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
    const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
      "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
    ).matches;

    if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
      const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
      const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
      const ease = 0.08;

      let mouseX = 0;
      let mouseY = 0;
      let hasMouse = false;

      const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
        el,
        depth: parseFloat(el.dataset.depth ?? "1"),
        currentX: 0,
        currentY: 0,
      }));

      window.addEventListener(
        "mousemove",
        (e) => {
          mouseX = e.clientX;
          mouseY = e.clientY;
          hasMouse = true;
        },
        { signal }
      );

      function tick() {
        for (const s of state) {
          let targetX = 0;
          let targetY = 0;

          if (hasMouse) {
            const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
            const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
            const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

            const dx = restX - mouseX;
            const dy = restY - mouseY;
            const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

            if (dist < influenceRadius) {
              const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
              const nx = dx / dist;
              const ny = dy / dist;
              targetX = nx * maxOffset * s.depth * falloff;
              targetY = ny * maxOffset * s.depth * falloff;
            }
          }

          s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
          s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
          s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
        }
        parallaxRafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      }

      parallaxRafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    // v18 — shared low-level alpha-channel scan, factored out of what
    // used to be `fitHotspotToAlpha`'s own inline pixel walk (see that
    // function further below — it's now a thin wrapper around this).
    // Returns a Promise of the image's real painted bounding box as 0-1
    // FRACTIONS of its own width/height (not pixels, not a DOM position),
    // or `null` if the image has no visible pixels or the scan can't run
    // (cross-origin canvas reads throw; caught below). Needed by TWO
    // different features now — hotspot sizing (below) and the front-
    // island top-padding compensation (v18, right after this) — so it's
    // shared instead of duplicated a third time, same reasoning as the
    // v12 refactor that first pulled this out of the palm tree code.
    function scanAlphaBBoxFraction(
      sourceImg: HTMLImageElement
    ): Promise<{ left: number; top: number; right: number; bottom: number } | null> {
      return new Promise((resolve) => {
        const run = () => {
          try {
            const w = sourceImg.naturalWidth;
            const h = sourceImg.naturalHeight;
            if (!w || !h) {
              resolve(null);
              return;
            }

            const canvas = document.createElement("canvas");
            canvas.width = w;
            canvas.height = h;
            const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
            if (!ctx) {
              resolve(null);
              return;
            }

            ctx.drawImage(sourceImg, 0, 0);
            const { data } = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, w, h);

            let minX = w;
            let minY = h;
            let maxX = 0;
            let maxY = 0;
            let found = false;
            const step = 2; // sample every other pixel on both axes — 4x fewer reads, plenty precise here

            for (let y = 0; y < h; y += step) {
              for (let x = 0; x < w; x += step) {
                const alpha = data[(y * w + x) * 4 + 3];
                if (alpha > 10) {
                  found = true;
                  if (x < minX) minX = x;
                  if (x > maxX) maxX = x;
                  if (y < minY) minY = y;
                  if (y > maxY) maxY = y;
                }
              }
            }

            if (!found) {
              resolve(null);
              return;
            }

            resolve({
              left: minX / w,
              top: minY / h,
              right: maxX / w,
              bottom: maxY / h,
            });
          } catch {
            // Cross-origin (or other) canvas read failure — resolve null
            // so callers fall back to their own CSS default rather than
            // breaking anything.
            resolve(null);
          }
        };

        if (sourceImg.complete) {
          run();
        } else {
          sourceImg.addEventListener("load", run, { once: true });
        }
      });
    }

    // v17 — keeps the front island clear of `.content` on desktop
    // (`min-width: 641px`), instead of the old fixed corner anchor that
    // could sit behind the text. `.content`'s height isn't fixed — it
    // moves with your actual title/body copy, viewport width (line-
    // wrapping), and the font-size `clamp()`s in the stylesheet — so a
    // hardcoded position would only be correct for one specific length
    // of text at one specific viewport, same problem `fitHotspotToAlpha`
    // below solves for image alignment, just for layout instead. Measures
    // `.content`'s real rendered bottom edge (relative to `.hobby-
    // section`) and positions `.hobby-island-group-front` a fixed gap
    // below that, clearing the CSS `bottom` anchor at the same time —
    // leaving both `top` and `bottom` set with no explicit height would
    // stretch the box to fill the space between them, breaking the
    // aspect-ratio-driven sizing on `.hobby-island-group-inner`. No-op
    // (and clears any inline styles from a previous wider viewport) below
    // the `min-width: 641px` breakpoint, where the mobile stylesheet
    // already stacks the front island below `.content` in normal flow —
    // nothing to compute there.
    //
    // v18 — "closer to the text" turned out to still barely move once you
    // saw it live, even after shrinking the gap from 32px to 4px. Cause:
    // `frontIslandImage`'s canvas has its own transparent padding above
    // the actual painted art (fountain/stairs/etc.) — the OLD math
    // positioned the top of that mostly-invisible BOX a fixed gap below
    // the text, so most of the "closer" from shrinking the gap was
    // closing empty space nobody could see, not moving the visible art.
    // Now `frontIslandBaseImg` gets alpha-scanned once (via
    // `scanAlphaBBoxFraction` above) to find exactly how much of the top
    // of its box is transparent padding, and that amount gets subtracted
    // from the computed `top` — so the position is based on where the
    // ART actually starts, not where its canvas happens to start. Same
    // "measure the real thing instead of guessing a percentage" reasoning
    // as v17 and every alpha-scan hotspot in this file.
    const hobbySection = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".hobby-section");
    const hobbyContent = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
      ".hobby-section > .content"
    );
    const frontGroup = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
      ".hobby-island-group-front"
    );
    const frontIslandBaseImg = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>(
      "[data-front-island-base]"
    );
    const desktopQuery = window.matchMedia("(min-width: 641px)");

    let frontIslandTopFraction = 0;
    const frontIslandAlphaReady = frontIslandBaseImg
      ? scanAlphaBBoxFraction(frontIslandBaseImg).then((bbox) => {
          if (bbox) frontIslandTopFraction = bbox.top;
        })
      : Promise.resolve();

    function positionFrontGroupBelowContent() {
      if (!hobbySection || !hobbyContent || !frontGroup) return;

      if (!desktopQuery.matches) {
        frontGroup.style.top = "";
        frontGroup.style.bottom = "";
        frontGroup.style.visibility = "";
        return;
      }

      const sectionRect = hobbySection.getBoundingClientRect();
      const contentRect = hobbyContent.getBoundingClientRect();
      // `frontGroup`'s own height comes from its `width` + the
      // `aspect-ratio` on `.hobby-island-group-inner` — reading it here
      // is safe regardless of the `top`/`bottom` this function is about
      // to set, since neither affects the box's own size.
      const groupHeight = frontGroup.getBoundingClientRect().height;
      const gap = -10; // px, breathing room between the text and the VISIBLE art below it — negative per your feedback, for a bit of intentional tuck rather than a visible gap
      const topPaddingPx = frontIslandTopFraction * groupHeight;

      frontGroup.style.top = `${
        contentRect.bottom - sectionRect.top + gap - topPaddingPx
      }px`;
      frontGroup.style.bottom = "auto";
      frontGroup.style.visibility = "visible";
    }

    // Fixed a visible "jumps lower right after load" glitch: the CSS
    // fallback position (`.hobby-island-group-front`'s plain `bottom`
    // rule, still in the stylesheet as a no-JS/no-op safety net) sits
    // noticeably higher than where this script ends up placing it, and
    // module scripts like this one run AFTER the browser has already
    // parsed/painted the initial HTML+CSS — so there was a real gap
    // between "page paints with the old corner-anchored position" and
    // "script runs and snaps it down to the new below-text position,"
    // which is exactly the jump you saw. The stylesheet now hides this
    // group by default on desktop (`visibility: hidden` under
    // `min-width: 641px`) so nothing paints there until this script has
    // actually measured and positioned it — `positionFrontGroupBelowContent`
    // sets `visibility: visible` itself once it runs, as the last step of
    // placing it correctly, so the FIRST thing ever shown is the right
    // position, not a flash of the old one. Also waits for
    // `document.fonts.ready` before that very first call (when the API is
    // available) — web fonts swapping in can change `.content`'s height
    // right after paint too, which would've caused this exact same kind
    // of jump a second time, just from a different cause. Subsequent
    // resizes/breakpoint changes call it directly (no need to wait for
    // fonts again) since the group is already visible by then and a
    // reposition while visible just tracks the text like it's meant to,
    // it doesn't "jump" from a stale spot the visitor never saw. v18: also
    // waits for `frontIslandAlphaReady` now, for the same reason — reveal
    // only once the top-padding compensation is known too, so the FIRST
    // visible position already accounts for it instead of jumping again
    // once the scan resolves a moment later.
    function showFrontGroupOnceReady() {
      const fontsReady = document.fonts?.ready
        ? document.fonts.ready.catch(() => {})
        : Promise.resolve();

      Promise.all([fontsReady, frontIslandAlphaReady]).then(
        positionFrontGroupBelowContent
      );
    }

    showFrontGroupOnceReady();
    window.addEventListener("resize", positionFrontGroupBelowContent, { signal });
    desktopQuery.addEventListener("change", positionFrontGroupBelowContent, { signal });

    // Shared by both hover hotspots below (palm tree, frost — v8 and
    // v12 respectively): sizes `hotspotEl` (an invisible
    // `pointer-events: auto` div) to the ACTUAL painted pixels of
    // `sourceImg`, not its full (mostly transparent) canvas-sized layer
    // box — otherwise hovering anywhere on that island would trigger the
    // effect, not just the specific artwork it belongs to. Needs
    // `sourceImg` to be same-origin (true for your site's own `/images/`
    // assets) since cross-origin canvas pixel reads throw; that's caught
    // inside `scanAlphaBBoxFraction` above, and the hotspot just falls
    // back to covering the whole slot (its CSS default) rather than
    // breaking anything. v18: now a thin wrapper around
    // `scanAlphaBBoxFraction` (see above) instead of its own copy of the
    // pixel-walk — same behavior/output as before, just de-duplicated
    // since that scan is now used in two places.
    function fitHotspotToAlpha(
      sourceImg: HTMLImageElement,
      hotspotEl: HTMLElement
    ) {
      scanAlphaBBoxFraction(sourceImg).then((bbox) => {
        if (!bbox) return;

        // Small padding so the hotspot isn't pixel-tight against the
        // artwork's edge — makes it a little more forgiving to hover.
        const padX = (bbox.right - bbox.left) * 0.05;
        const padY = (bbox.bottom - bbox.top) * 0.05;
        const left = Math.max(0, bbox.left - padX);
        const top = Math.max(0, bbox.top - padY);
        const right = Math.min(1, bbox.right + padX);
        const bottom = Math.min(1, bbox.bottom + padY);

        hotspotEl.style.left = `${left * 100}%`;
        hotspotEl.style.top = `${top * 100}%`;
        hotspotEl.style.width = `${(right - left) * 100}%`;
        hotspotEl.style.height = `${(bottom - top) * 100}%`;
      });
    }

    // v27 — fountain sparkle-highlight positioning (see the v27 comment
    // near `.hobby-fountain-sparkle` in the stylesheet for the visual
    // design). Each `.hobby-fountain-sparkle-dot` carries `data-fx`/
    // `data-fy`, a hand-picked position as a fraction (0-1) of the
    // fountain's OWN painted area — "20% across, 25% down the water",
    // not "20% across the whole padded canvas". First attempt at this
    // used flat canvas-fraction percentages directly and genuinely
    // reproduced v22's "zero pixel difference" mistake: nearly every dot
    // landed in the transparent padding around the small fountain
    // artwork, not on the water itself, since the shared canvas is
    // mostly empty space (same convention noted throughout this file).
    // Fixed the same way v18's front-island padding and both hover
    // hotspots were: alpha-scan the actual art (`scanAlphaBBoxFraction`
    // above) to find its real bounding box as a fraction of the canvas,
    // then map each dot's fountain-relative fraction into that box
    // before setting real `top`/`left` percentages. Runs once
    // `fountainImg` has loaded; no-ops (dots stay at their CSS default,
    // dead center) if the scan can't run at all.
    const fountainImg = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>(
      "[data-fountain-image]"
    );
    const fountainSparkleDots = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(
      "[data-fountain-sparkle] .hobby-fountain-sparkle-dot"
    );

    if (fountainImg && fountainSparkleDots.length) {
      scanAlphaBBoxFraction(fountainImg).then((bbox) => {
        if (!bbox) return;

        const width = bbox.right - bbox.left;
        const height = bbox.bottom - bbox.top;

        fountainSparkleDots.forEach((dot) => {
          const fx = parseFloat(dot.dataset.fx || "0.5");
          const fy = parseFloat(dot.dataset.fy || "0.5");
          dot.style.left = `${(bbox.left + fx * width) * 100}%`;
          dot.style.top = `${(bbox.top + fy * height) * 100}%`;
        });
      });
    }

    // Palm tree hover swap (see the v8 comment at the top of the file).
    // No-ops entirely if `palmTreeImage` wasn't passed (no
    // `[data-palm-base]` element on the page); the hover/reveal itself
    // is plain CSS (`:hover` + `clip-path` transition, see the
    // stylesheet), so there's nothing else to wire up here even when
    // `palmTree80sImage` IS present.
    const palmBase = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>(
      "[data-palm-base]"
    );
    const palmHotspot = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
      "[data-palm-hotspot]"
    );

    if (palmBase && palmHotspot) {
      fitHotspotToAlpha(palmBase, palmHotspot);
    }

    // Back island frost hover reveal (see the v12 comment at the top of
    // the file). Same mechanism as the palm tree above, just scanning
    // `backIslandFrozenImage` itself instead of a separate "base" layer,
    // since the frost art IS the thing you're hovering to reveal — the
    // hotspot ends up sized to wherever the frost actually paints, not
    // the whole back island. No-ops if `backIslandFrozenImage` wasn't
    // passed (no `[data-frozen-overlay]` element on the page); like the
    // palm tree, the reveal itself is plain CSS now, nothing further to
    // wire up here.
    const frozenOverlay = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>(
      "[data-frozen-overlay]"
    );
    const frostHotspot = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
      "[data-frost-hotspot]"
    );

    if (frozenOverlay && frostHotspot) {
      fitHotspotToAlpha(frozenOverlay, frostHotspot);
    }

    // Monado sword symbol cycling (see the v14 comment at the top of
    // the file). All symbol images share the same painted position (an
    // `inset: 0` layer like every other artwork here — see v6), so this
    // is pure show/hide bookkeeping, no positioning math needed: exactly
    // one `.hobby-monado-symbol` carries `.is-active` at a time, which
    // is what triggers its CSS flicker-in animation (see the
    // stylesheet). No-ops if `monadoImage`/`monadoSymbolImages` weren't
    // passed (no `[data-monado-symbol]` elements on the page).
    const monadoSymbols = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(
      "[data-monado-symbol]"
    );

    if (monadoSymbols.length) {
      const symbols = Array.from(monadoSymbols);
      let currentIndex = -1;
      monadoTimeoutHolder.id = null;

      const pickNextIndex = () => {
        if (symbols.length === 1) return 0;
        let next = currentIndex;
        while (next === currentIndex) {
          next = Math.floor(Math.random() * symbols.length);
        }
        return next;
      };

      const showSymbol = (index: number) => {
        if (currentIndex >= 0) {
          symbols[currentIndex].classList.remove("is-active");
        }
        currentIndex = index;
        symbols[currentIndex].classList.add("is-active");
      };

      // Show one immediately (rather than waiting for the first random
      // delay) so the sword's gem is never empty on page load — reduced
      // motion also relies on this: it never reschedules below, so this
      // first call is the ONLY thing that ever sets a symbol for those
      // visitors, and `.is-active`'s animation is disabled entirely in
      // that case (see the stylesheet), so it just appears, no flicker.
      showSymbol(pickNextIndex());

      if (!prefersReducedMotion) {
        const minDelay = 3000; // ms, shortest gap between symbol changes
        const maxDelay = 7000; // ms, longest gap between symbol changes

        const scheduleNext = () => {
          const delay = minDelay + Math.random() * (maxDelay - minDelay);
          monadoTimeoutHolder.id = setTimeout(() => {
            showSymbol(pickNextIndex());
            scheduleNext();
          }, delay);
        };

        scheduleNext();
      }
    }

    cleanupHobby = () => {
      controller.abort();
      if (parallaxRafId !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(parallaxRafId);
      if (monadoTimeoutHolder.id !== null) clearTimeout(monadoTimeoutHolder.id);
    };
  });
</script>

<style>
