    clip-path: circle(0% at 50% 50%);
    /* v13: slowed from 0.6s (the palm tree's speed, which this
       borrowed at first) to 1.1s, per your feedback — reads more like
       frost actually spreading/withdrawing rather than a quick UI
       swap. v16: slowed again, from 1.1s to 1.8s, per further
       feedback. Deliberately NOT touching `.hobby-palm-base`/
       `.hobby-palm-80s`'s 0.6s — you didn't ask to slow those down,
       and they're a different kind of effect (a tree swap, not a
       spreading texture) where the quicker pace still fits. */
    transition: clip-path 1.8s ease;
  }

  /* Same reasoning as `.hobby-palm-hotspot` (v8): opts back into
     `pointer-events: auto` despite `.hobby-island-group`'s blanket
     `pointer-events: none`, which is what makes it hoverable at all.
     `inset: 0` is only the fallback until the script above sizes it to
     the frost art's real bounding box. Needs to come BEFORE
     `.hobby-frozen-overlay` in the markup for `~` below to reach it. */
  .hobby-frost-hotspot {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  /* 150% deliberately overshoots — for a `circle()` radius without a
     `farthest-corner` keyword, 100% doesn't reliably reach the corners
     of a non-square box, and overshooting costs nothing here since a
     circle bigger than the image just means "fully visible," same as
     any other value past full coverage. */
  .hobby-frost-hotspot:hover ~ .hobby-frozen-overlay {
    clip-path: circle(150% at 50% 50%);
  }

  /* Palm tree hover swap (see the v8/v9 comments at the top of the
     file). `.hobby-palm-base` (`palmTreeImage`) and `.hobby-palm-80s`
     (`palmTree80sImage`) occupy the exact same box and use MATCHING
     `inset()` clip-paths run over the same duration/easing, moving in
     the same direction (top → bottom) — that's deliberate, not just
     two independent wipes. At any instant during the transition, the
     region `.hobby-palm-80s` has revealed so far is EXACTLY the region
     `.hobby-palm-base` has clipped away so far, so exactly one of the
     two is ever visible at a given point on the tree — no gap, and
     critically (per your feedback) no double-exposure where bits of
     the normal tree's silhouette peek out from behind the
     differently-shaped 80s art. It reads as a single scanning
     replacement sweeping down, not a cross-fade. */
  .hobby-palm-base,
  .hobby-palm-80s {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transition: clip-path 0.6s ease;
  }

  .hobby-palm-base {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0);
  }

  .hobby-palm-80s {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 100% 0);
  }

  /* Invisible, positioned at runtime by the script above (via inline
     `left`/`top`/`width`/`height`) to the real painted pixels of the
     palm tree — `inset: 0` here is only the fallback used until that
     script runs (or if it can't, e.g. a cross-origin image). Opts
     back into `pointer-events: auto` despite `.hobby-island-group`
     setting `pointer-events: none` on everything else in this
     section, which is what makes it hoverable at all. Also doubles as
     `.hobby-palm-scanline`'s positioning parent now (v10) — see below. */
  .hobby-palm-hotspot {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  /* `~` reaches forward from the hotspot to these two — this only
     works because the hotspot comes FIRST in the markup (general
     sibling combinators can't look backwards; it's ahead of
     `.hobby-palm-base` specifically so it could reach that one too).
     Un-hovering just transitions everything back to its rest state
     above, so the whole swap reverses on its own with no extra rule
     needed. */
  .hobby-palm-hotspot:hover ~ .hobby-palm-base {
    clip-path: inset(100% 0 0 0);
  }

  /* v11 — layered a `filter` flicker on TOP of the clip-path
     transition (an `animation` running alongside it, not replacing
     it — they're independent properties, so this doesn't touch the
     v9 sync guarantee at all). Only on the way IN: the 80s art
     stutters through a couple of brightness/hue spikes as it locks
     in, like a signal settling, rather than smoothly brightening in —
     the normal tree still just clips cleanly away with no flicker of
     its own, so the "electronic" cue reads as the new image arriving
     rather than the old one glitching out. */
  .hobby-palm-hotspot:hover ~ .hobby-palm-80s {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0% 0);
    animation: hobby-palm-glitch 0.6s steps(14, end);
  }

  @keyframes hobby-palm-glitch {
    0%, 100% { filter: none; }
    10% { filter: brightness(1.7) saturate(1.5); }
    18% { filter: none; }
    34% { filter: brightness(0.6) hue-rotate(12deg); }
    42% { filter: none; }
    68% { filter: brightness(1.35); }
    78% { filter: none; }
  }

  /* v10 — per your feedback: (1) the line was rendering across the
     hotspot's OLD position, `.hobby-layer-drift`'s full `inset: 0` box
     (the whole front island, mostly empty canvas around the actual
     tree) instead of just the tree — fixed by making the scanline a
     CHILD of `.hobby-palm-hotspot` instead of a sibling reached via
     `~`. The hotspot is the element the script above already resizes
     to the tree's real painted bounding box, so nesting the scanline
     inside it for free makes `left/right: 0` and `top: 0%..100%`
     resolve against that same tight box instead of the whole slot —
     no separate coordinates to compute or keep in sync. (`:hover` here
     is a plain descendant combinator, not `~`, since it's a child now.)
     (2) thinned it from a soft 0.6%-tall gradient bar down to two
     crisp 1.5px hairlines. (3) "more electronic": split it into an
     offset cyan/magenta pair (RGB channels visibly mis-registered by a
     couple pixels — the CRT/VHS chromatic-aberration cue) and swapped
     the smooth `linear` sweep for `steps()`, so it jumps down in
     discrete increments like a hardware scanner or LED readout instead
     of gliding like a physical object. */
  .hobby-palm-scanline {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    height: 1.5px;
    background: #ff2fd6;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0.3em 0 rgba(255, 47, 214, 0.85);
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* The cyan "ghost" channel, offset a couple pixels above the magenta
     line above — together they read as one RGB-fringed scan line
     rather than two, without needing a second real element in the
     markup. */
  .hobby-palm-scanline::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: -2px;
    height: 1.5px;
    background: #00f6ff;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0.3em 0 rgba(0, 246, 255, 0.85);
  }

  .hobby-palm-hotspot:hover .hobby-palm-scanline {
    animation: hobby-palm-scan 0.6s steps(14, end);
  }

  /* v11: added a horizontal `translateX` jitter on top of the vertical
     sweep — small, irregularly-spaced left/right kicks rather than a
     steady wobble, closer to signal noise/interference than a
     deliberate side-to-side motion. Still driven by the same
     `steps()` timing as the vertical movement, so the jitter jumps
     between fixed offsets exactly like the sweep jumps between
     positions, instead of smoothly sliding — keeps the whole thing
     reading as one consistently "digital" motion instead of a smooth
     wobble bolted onto a stepped sweep. */
  @keyframes hobby-palm-scan {
    0% { top: 0%; opacity: 0; transform: translateX(0); }
    8% { opacity: 1; }
    14% { transform: translateX(-3px); }
    27% { transform: translateX(2px); }
    41% { transform: translateX(-2px); }
    56% { transform: translateX(3px); }
    63% { transform: translateX(0); }
    79% { transform: translateX(-2px); }
    92% { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); }
    100% { top: 100%; opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* v14 — Monado sword: `.hobby-monado` is just another plain stacked
     layer, no special behavior (it's LAST among the front island's
     children, i.e. on TOP of `frontIslandImage`/the palm tree, per
     "in front of it"). The symbols are the interesting part: all five
     of yours share the exact same painted position (checked — same
     bounding box on every file), so unlike the palm tree/frost this
     needed no hotspot or alignment math, just show/hide bookkeeping —
     exactly one `.hobby-monado-symbol` has `.is-active` at a time
     (script above), everything else stays at `opacity: 0`. */
  .hobby-monado,
  .hobby-monado-symbol {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .hobby-monado-symbol {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* A quick flicker-in rather than a plain cross-fade — matches the
     "electronic" language established for the palm tree (v10/v11):
     `steps()` instead of a smooth curve, and the opacity/brightness
     bouncing partway through rather than rising in one motion, so it
     reads as the gem re-igniting with the new symbol rather than
     smoothly dissolving into it. The OUTGOING symbol isn't animated at
     all — the script removes its `.is-active` class in the same tick
     it adds the new one's, so it snaps to `opacity: 0` instantly with
     no `transition` declared here to soften that. That's deliberate:
     softening the outgoing symbol would mean two symbols visibly
     overlapping mid-swap, the same double-exposure problem solved for
     the palm tree in v9 — instant-out + flicker-in avoids it without
     needing any clip-path sync trick this time, since a hard cut on
     the way out never overlaps the incoming animation at all. */
  .hobby-monado-symbol.is-active {
    animation: hobby-monado-symbol-in 0.5s steps(8, end) forwards;
  }

  @keyframes hobby-monado-symbol-in {
    0% { opacity: 0; filter: brightness(2) saturate(1.6); }
    20% { opacity: 1; }
    35% { opacity: 0.2; filter: brightness(0.6); }
    50% { opacity: 1; filter: brightness(1.8); }
    65% { opacity: 0.3; }
    100% { opacity: 1; filter: none; }
  }

  /* v19 — fountain water-flow effect: `fountainImage` renders as a
     stacked layer on top of `frontIslandImage`, and `.hobby-fountain-
     flow` is a SEPARATE overlay on top of THAT — a repeating pattern of
     streaks whose `background-position` loops downward, giving the
     "lines flowing down" look you asked for. The key trick is `mask-
     image` (with the `-webkit-` prefix for Safari): set to the SAME
     `fountainImage` URL via the `--fountain-mask` custom property (set
     inline per-instance in the markup, so this rule doesn't need to
     know the actual path), it clips this overlay to exactly the
     water's own alpha shape — so the streaks only ever appear ON the
     water, never spilling onto the surrounding island art, no matter
     how the fountain is shaped or re-exported. `mix-blend-mode: screen`
     lightens rather than flatly overlaying, so it reads as light
     catching moving water rather than opaque white bars painted on
     top. Purely decorative/ambient (see the `prefers-reduced-motion`
     block further down), same treatment as the palm tree scanline.

     v19 fix — `.hobby-fountain-image` needs `position: absolute; inset:
     0` explicitly, same as `.hobby-palm-base`/`.hobby-monado` above:
     without it, the plain `.hobby-layer` class only sets `display:
     block; width: 100%`, so the fountain `<img>` stayed in NORMAL FLOW
     and stacked below `frontIslandImage` instead of overlaying it. Now
     fixed, so it overlays exactly on top like every other layer here.

     v19 tweak — darker/more saturated water, sharper streaks, per your
     feedback: `.hobby-fountain-image` gets a `filter` (`saturate` +
     `brightness` down a touch) applied directly to your art, no re-
     export needed.

     v20 — the streaks were still ruler-straight bands; per your
     feedback ("a bit curved... more realistic"), the background is no
     longer a `repeating-linear-gradient` at all — it's a small tiling
     SVG (inlined as a data URI, one gentle wave-shaped stroke per
     tile). The stroke itself is crisp/vector (no blur, keeping the v19
     "sharp lines" feedback), just curved instead of straight.

     v20 tweak — per your reference image, one wide shallow arc (like a
     smile), not several small ripples. First attempt stretched a
     single arc across the FULL front-island box width via `background-
     size: 100%` + `repeat-y` — but the fountain's own visible area is
     only a small slice of that full box (same "shared canvas, mostly
     transparent padding" convention as every other layer here), so
     what showed through the mask was just a thin, nearly-flat sliver
     of that much bigger arc — barely visible. A fixed-pixel-guess tile
     next (closer, but occasionally showed two partial arcs instead of
     one clean one), then a runtime alpha-scan of the fountain art to
     size/align that tile exactly — technically precise, but you still
     said it "doesn't look right" once you saw it live.

     v21 — at that point we'd tried three different LINE shapes
     (straight, rippled, one wide arc) without success, so per your
     invitation to just pick something that looks good, this drops the
     "line tracing the water" concept entirely. Real light on water
     doesn't read as lines so much as soft, shifting patches of
     brightness — so this is now a handful of `radial-gradient`
     highlights (no SVG) that drift to different positions over a slow
     `ease-in-out` loop, via plain `background-position` keyframes on
     each gradient layer independently. Same masking approach as every
     version before (`mask-image` to the fountain's own alpha shape via
     `--fountain-mask`, `mix-blend-mode: screen` so it lightens rather
     than paints flat white) — only the shape/motion of the highlights
     changed. No runtime measurement needed this time: soft drifting
     blobs look right at any size or position within the mask, unlike a
     line that has to trace a specific edge.

     v21 tweak — per your feedback, an occasional hard-looking edge:
     `fountainImage`'s alpha shape is presumably a crisp vector cutout
     with no feathering of its own, and `mask-image` clips strictly to
     that alpha with no softening, so whenever a highlight's brightest
     point happened to drift right up against the water's silhouette,
     that hard mask boundary became visible as a sharp line. Two
     changes: lowered each gradient's peak opacity (0.95/0.8/0.7 down
     to 0.6/0.5/0.42) so any given point has less contrast against the
     cutoff to begin with, and added `filter: blur(5px)` to the whole
     layer — smooths the highlights' own edges BEFORE the mask clips
     them, so what reaches the hard boundary is already soft rather
     than a crisp gradient stop. Also pulled the drift keyframes in
     from the very edges of the box toward the center, so a highlight's
     bright core sits near the water's boundary less often to begin
     with.

     v21 tweak 2 — all three highlights were plain white at different
     opacities, so `mix-blend-mode: screen` just lightened the water by
     different AMOUNTS — same hue shift each time, hard to tell apart
     as they crossed. First fix picked three "shades of blue" that
     were still too close in hue to each other to read as different
     once screened over the water (screen pushes any light color
     toward white fast on an already-saturated backdrop, so similar
     inputs converged to similar, barely-distinguishable results). Re-
     picked with real distance between them (violet-blue, cyan-teal,
     pale near-white-blue) — still too subtle once you saw it live,
     because `screen` is fundamentally a BRIGHTENING blend: it can only
     push channels up, and against a backdrop whose blue channel is
     already near maximum, every highlight color converges toward the
     same pale/white result almost regardless of its own hue — the
     mode itself was working against the goal, not just the color
     choices. Switched to `mix-blend-mode: color` instead, which
     replaces the backdrop's hue/saturation with the source's while
     keeping the backdrop's own shading/luminance — so each highlight
     now visibly RECOLORS the water it passes over (to that highlight's
     actual hue) rather than just brightening it, which is a much
     bigger, unambiguous difference between the three than any amount
     of screen-blended color tuning could give. Gradient stops bumped
     to fully opaque at their center (the falloff to transparent at the
     edge, plus the blur, still keeps it soft) since `color` blend
     doesn't have `screen`'s natural "converges toward white" ceiling
     to lean on for softness.

     v22 — first attempt at "something more creative" was concentric
     ripple rings/pulses (`.hobby-fountain-ripple`, mask + `mix-blend-
     mode: color`, alpha-scanned to center on the water). Diagnosed it
     properly this time — paused every OTHER animation on the page and
     diffed a frame with the effect against the same frame with it
     forced invisible — and the honest result was a real ZERO pixel
     difference, not just "subtle": at the sizes/positions that
     approach produced, the visible content never actually overlapped
     the water in a way that showed. Rather than keep tuning numbers
